WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:09.607
Higgsfield credits, decoded. And straight away, an update: as of August twenty twenty-six, we no longer recommend Higgsfield.

2
00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:22.077
This walkthrough is kept for anyone who already holds credits or a pass, and for the lesson underneath it, which applies to any platform that sells generating power by the credit.

3
00:00:22.777 --> 00:00:31.310
What we use now is fal dot A I, and there are two reasons. It costs less for the same work. And the API is better to build against.

4
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:46.359
That second one is the half that bites hardest here, because the traffic this guide is about, generations coming from a script or an agent rather than a person clicking, is precisely the traffic Higgsfield's passes refuse to cover.

5
00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:55.935
So start new work on fal dot A I. Everything after this point is the analysis as it stood, which is still accurate about Higgsfield's own products.

6
00:00:56.635 --> 00:01:05.788
Here is the clause, quoted from the offer itself. Standard generation modes only. Excludes MCP, Supercomputer, Canvas and CLI.

7
00:01:05.788 --> 00:01:16.539
And standard generation modes means a human, sitting in the web app, clicking Generate. It does not mean the API, an MCP server or the command line.

8
00:01:16.539 --> 00:01:29.979
So if your generations come from a script, an agent or an integration rather than a person clicking, none of your usage is covered, and it bills exactly as it would with no pass at all.

9
00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:36.983
Now something worth getting straight before any of the maths. The plan does not change the price.

10
00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:47.967
Every generation costs credits, whatever plan you are on. What the plan changes is how many credits you get and at what rate. It does not change what a generation costs.

11
00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:57.521
So the question is never which plan, first. It is: how many credits will this project actually burn, and then what is the cheapest way to buy them?

12
00:01:58.221 --> 00:02:02.307
And where does the money actually go? Video, by a wide margin.

13
00:02:02.307 --> 00:02:13.180
A single five-second clip at ten-eighty p costs roughly the same as eleven high-quality stills. Voiceover takes are close to a rounding error next to either of them.

14
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:28.732
On a real production day, measured end to end from the billing ledger, the video clips alone were about eighty per cent of the spend. So any project that leans on clips rather than stills should be budgeted on that basis from the start.

15
00:02:29.432 --> 00:02:43.715
There is one habit that pays for itself immediately. Higgsfield's own MCP calls and command-line tool both accept a cost flag, which returns the exact credit cost for a generation without submitting it.

16
00:02:43.715 --> 00:02:48.524
Check that before a batch, rather than trusting a rate you remember.

17
00:02:48.524 --> 00:02:57.574
Costs and models both change, and a remembered rate is precisely how a batch runs three times over budget before anyone notices.

18
00:02:58.274 --> 00:03:04.606
Now, suppose for a moment that a pass did cover automated traffic. Does the arithmetic even work?

19
00:03:04.606 --> 00:03:20.010
The day pass carries its own concurrency cap of one video at a time. So hitting break-even inside twenty-four hours, one clip at a time, each taking several minutes to render, sits very close to the physical ceiling of what is possible.

20
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:29.018
Which is worth knowing, because it means the exclusion clause is not the only reason a pass loses on automated work. It would lose anyway.

21
00:03:29.718 --> 00:03:38.451
To be fair to the passes, there is a case where they genuinely win. A human sitting in the web app, generating at volume by hand.

22
00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:45.018
Mass product photography. Bulk b-roll. High-volume image work with no automation involved at all.

23
00:03:45.018 --> 00:03:57.814
The longer passes are priced against sustained daily volume rather than a single burst, and used that way they can be excellent value. The exclusion only bites when the traffic is scripted.

24
00:03:58.514 --> 00:04:09.589
Then there is the third product, Concurrency Boost Credits. These add parallel generation slots and queue priority, at roughly half the credits-per-pound of a plain top-up.

25
00:04:09.589 --> 00:04:16.608
So the premium is buying concurrency, not a cheaper credit, and that is the thing to keep clear in your head.

26
00:04:16.608 --> 00:04:26.009
They earn that premium when the constraint genuinely is the queue: large same-day batches where you need far more parallel jobs to hit a deadline.

27
00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:38.824
They do not help at all when the bottleneck is somewhere else, which for careful production work it usually is: reviewing outputs, retaking rejected frames, writing prompts, assembling the final cut.

28
00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:49.629
And one last trap before the close. Boosting your parallel job limit does not lift the one-video-at-a-time cap that applies while a pass is active.

29
00:04:49.629 --> 00:04:58.153
They are different products with different rules, and they do not stack the way the marketing copy might lead you to assume.

30
00:04:58.153 --> 00:05:12.590
Which is the whole lesson of this page, and the reason it is worth keeping even now: the pricing was never the hard part. The small print is. Read the exclusion clauses before you buy anything, on any platform.

31
00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:17.883
And for new work, we now recommend fal dot A I: lower cost, and a better API.

